data: {"step": "response", "status": "streaming", "detail": "For the officer joining this caseload, key considerations include:  \n\n- **District focus**: Cases are concentrated in **\u0641\u06cc\u0635\u0644 \u0622\u0628\u0627\u062f** (Faisalabad: 19 cases), **\u0644\u0627\u06c1\u0648\u0631** (Lahore: 18 cases), and **\u0631\u0627\u0648\u0644\u067e\u0646\u0688\u06cc** (Rawalpindi: 10 cases), with smaller numbers in other districts [Document 1].  \n- **Case mix**: Compared to 2024, **PPC cases have surged to 39** (up from 13), while **Arms Ordinance 1965 cases decreased to 16** (from 13). New categories include **CNSA 1997 (12)**, **PECA 2016 (9)**, and **Punjab Domestic Violence Act (4)**, reflecting diversification [Document 2].  \n- **Reporting delays**: The mean time from incident to report is **1401.3 minutes (~23.4 hours)** in 2026, significantly slower than **15.0 minutes** in 2024 [Document 4].  \n- **Arrest rates**: Only **11 FIRs** (out of 73) record arrests, while **62 FIRs** have no arrests, with many accused marked as \"under investigation\" or \"absconding\" [Document 3].  \n- **Weapon licensing**: **30 cases** involve recovered weapons with **no licence recorded**, and **2 cases** have **no licence status at all** [Document 5].  \n- **Accused profile**: **67 male** and **24 female** accused (total 94), with **3 cases** having unknown gender [Document 6].  \n\nPrioritize districts with high case volumes, prepare for delayed reporting and low arrest rates, and address unlicensed weapons and emerging legal issues like PECA 2016 and CNSA 1997."}

